<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">enchanting</ansi>

Hunting pays in gear as well as gold. Creatures drop <ansi fg="yellow">enchanting trophies</ansi>
(hearts, hides, chitin, ash) by the kind of creature they are, and an
<ansi fg="yellow">enchanter</ansi> will work one into a weapon or piece of armor for a fee. The
piece then gives the trophy's small effect for as long as it is worn, by you
or by a companion. (The heavy hides and tusks sold at markets are trade
goods, not these: <ansi fg="command">help goods</ansi>.)

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">imbue</ansi>
  At an enchanter: lists the trophies you carry and what each would give.
  Enchanters stand in towns; look for the <ansi fg="purple">enchanter</ansi> tag beside a name (the web
  client's quick menu offers Enchant gear under Services).

  <ansi fg="command">imbue [item] with [trophy]</ansi>
  Works the trophy into the item, worn or carried. It costs 25 gold for each
  tier of the piece (a Common piece is tier 1, a Masterwork tier 4), spends the
  trophy and cannot be done in a battle. A companion's piece must be in your
  pack first (<ansi fg="command">help companion-gear</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Which creature drops which trophy</ansi>

  Beast hide     canines, beasts, rodents, hares, reptiles    +1% damage reduction
  Brute's heart  ogres, trolls, goblins                       +1 damage on every landed blow
  Chitin plate   giant spiders, insects                       +2 Evasion
  Grave ash      the undead, ghostly spirits, demons          +3% spell damage
  Hollow heart   eldritch horrors, trees, fungi               +2 Attack

Each company that fights makes its own roll when a creature falls: about 1
kill in 5 for a beast, brute or horror, 1 in 4 for the rest, twice that for
an elite, and a boss always gives one. Foes in the training yard drop none.
Once you have beaten a kind enough, <ansi fg="command">bestiary [name]</ansi> says which trophies it
may yield (<ansi fg="command">help bestiary</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Rules</ansi>

  - One trophy to a piece, and it is permanent. A second one is refused, so
    pick the piece you will keep.
  - Weapons and anything worn qualify; packs, trophies and quest items do not.
  - A relic may be enchanted too, but its signature, awakenings and the
    enchant together never pass what that effect may ever reach
    (<ansi fg="command">help relics</ansi>); an enchant that would is trimmed.
  - Every enchant is small, and one wearer's enchants do not stack: all of
    them together give no more of an effect than one trophy of the best kind
    (+2 Attack, +2 Evasion, +1 damage, +1% damage reduction, +3% spell
    damage). Give each piece a different trophy, so a fully enchanted member
    gains a little of several things.
  - It follows the item into the pack and a companion's gear. Company cargo
    refuses an enchanted piece, which would lose its enchant there.
  - If a relic already holds all of what a trophy gives, the enchanter
    refuses it, and you keep the trophy and your gold.
  - <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> at a piece, or hover it in the gear window, to read its enchant. In a
    battle, <ansi fg="command">why</ansi> names an enchant that raised a blow (<ansi fg="command">help why</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What it is worth</ansi>

An enchant adds nothing to what a merchant pays for the piece. The fee and
the trophy are spent for the power, not for resale, so an enchanted piece never
sells for more than its parts and fee cost. A trophy itself is a drop and is
worth a few coins.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help bestiary</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help relics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help goods</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help salvage</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>.
